## Deploy to Vercel

This repo is pre-wired for Vercel — `vercel.json` configures security headers, immutable asset caching, and production redirects. Pick **one** of the two flows below.

### Option A — Dashboard import (recommended for first deploy)

1. Push the repo to GitHub (or GitLab / Bitbucket).
2. In Vercel, click **Add New → Project**, then **Import** this repository.
3. Vercel auto-detects the framework as **Next.js**. Confirm:
    - Build Command: `next build`
    - Output Directory: `.next`
    - Install Command: `npm install`
4. (Optional) Add an environment variable:
    - `NEXT_PUBLIC_BRAND_URL` = `https://your-custom-domain.example`
5. Click **Deploy**. Subsequent pushes to your default branch auto-deploy to production.

### Option B — Vercel CLI (good for scripted deploys)

1. **Install the CLI and log in**

    ```bash
    npm i -g vercel
    vercel login
    ```

2. **Link the project (first time only)**

    ```bash
    vercel link
    ```

3. **Deploy a preview**

    ```bash
    vercel
    ```

4. **Deploy to production**

    ```bash
    vercel --prod
    ```

5. **Set the canonical URL (optional)**

    ```bash
    vercel env add NEXT_PUBLIC_BRAND_URL production
    # paste https://your-custom-domain.example when prompted
    vercel --prod   # redeploy to pick up the env var
    ```

### Verifying a successful deploy

After the deploy finishes:

- Open the production URL — the landing page renders with the navbar, hero, features, how-it-works, personas, FAQ, final CTA, and footer.
- Visit `/sitemap.xml` — returns valid XML referencing your production host.
- Visit `/robots.txt` — returns the production allow/deny rules and a `Sitemap:` line.
- Inspect response headers (`curl -I https://your-domain/`):
    - `strict-transport-security`, `x-content-type-options: nosniff`, `x-frame-options: SAMEORIGIN`, `referrer-policy`, and `permissions-policy` should all be present.
- Run a social-share validator:
    - Facebook Sharing Debugger: <https://developers.facebook.com/tools/debug/>
    - Twitter Card Validator: <https://cards-dev.twitter.com/validator>
    - LinkedIn Post Inspector: <https://www.linkedin.com/post-inspector/>

## Custom domain

1. In Vercel, open **Project Settings → Domains**.
2. Add your apex domain and follow the DNS instructions (A record or `ALIAS` for apex; `CNAME` for subdomains).
3. Vercel auto-issues a Let's Encrypt certificate.
4. Set `NEXT_PUBLIC_BRAND_URL=https://your-domain` in **Project Settings → Environment Variables**, then redeploy.

## SEO checklist

- [x] `<title>` and `<meta name="description">` set in `app/layout.tsx`
- [x] Open Graph + Twitter Card metadata in `app/layout.tsx`
- [x] Canonical URL via `alternates.canonical` in `lib/metadata.ts`
- [x] `public/sitemap.xml` references production host
- [x] `public/robots.txt` allows crawling and points to the sitemap
- [x] `public/og-image.svg` (and PNG variant) at the site root
- [x] Semantic landmarks (`<header>`, `<main>`, `<footer>`, `aria-labelledby`)
- [x] Skip-to-content link for keyboard users
- [ ] Submit sitemap to Google Search Console after first deploy
- [ ] Submit sitemap to Bing Webmaster Tools after first deploy

## Troubleshooting

- **Wrong canonical URL on the live site** — make sure `NEXT_PUBLIC_BRAND_URL` is set in the Vercel project settings, not just locally, and re-deploy.
- **OG image not updating in social previews** — social crawlers cache aggressively. Use the validator links above and append `?v=2` to the OG URL to bust caches.
- **`/sitemap.xml` returning 404** — confirm `public/sitemap.xml` exists; Vercel serves anything under `public/` at the site root.
- **Security header missing** — `vercel.json` is applied at the edge on every deploy. If a header disappears after a manual change in the dashboard, the dashboard value overrides the file — re-apply in the dashboard or revert there.

